977:  oxp-1-data-lock-office-seat-schema: done                    # STORY CREATED 2026-08-13, preceded by a real bmad-data-lock pass against live tm_suite MongoDB (not assumption) - see data-map.md. Two corrections to the epic's own premise: (1) game_sessions is NOT uniquely keyed by game_number - game_number 3 and 4 each have 5 documents, 1 real + 4 junk placeholders, and for game 3 the real one is the OLDEST not newest (data-map.md Known Drift Pattern #17); (2) seat multiplicity is NOT confined to Socialite - Primogen also currently has two live simultaneous holders (Yusuf Kalusicj, Rene St. Dominique), ruled REAL (not a data-hygiene bug) by Angelus 2026-08-13 ("you can have more than one Primogen") - the epic's "only Socialite has two seats" framing is itself corrected. Story scoped to: new office_seats collection (schema + AJV validation), seat-keyed generically (N per office, no Socialite special-case), seeded with the 7 real current seats (character ObjectIds already confirmed live, in the story's own Dev Notes table) - one seat's creation date (Rene's second Primogen seat) is an open data question flagged for whoever runs the seed script, not guessed. Deliberately does NOT migrate office_merit_dots/office_manoeuvre_rank to seat-keying (their oxp-3/oxp-4-era "no seat ambiguity problem" reasoning is now proven wrong live - Socialite's two real seats would collide the moment either gets a real purchase - but neither has yet, so this is recorded as a known gap, not fixed speculatively). Does NOT include a new API route or any UI consumption - nothing reads office_seats yet, that's oxp.2/oxp.6/oxp.7's job. OFFICE_DATA's static-JS-to-Mongo migration (also bundled into the epic's original oxp.1 text) split out to a new oxp-10 entry below. Story: oxp-1-data-lock-office-seat-schema.md DEV COMPLETE 2026-08-13, awaiting code review: new server/schemas/office_seat.schema.js (seat-keyed, N seats per office, required-but-nullable 24-hex ObjectId holder_id, pattern-checked ISO created_at) + server/scripts/seed-office-seats.mjs (manual one-off, dry-run by default, idempotent on {office_category, holder_id}, refuses to build without Rene St. Dominique's still-unconfirmed creation date rather than defaulting it) + server/tests/oxp-1-office-seats.test.js (41 tests, 9 of them DB-backed against tm_suite_test). 112/112 across 6 files, zero skipped; four gates prove-discriminated by mutation. The live tm_suite database was never connected to or written to; the seed script has NOT been run for real and must not be until Rene's date is confirmed with Angelus. CODE REVIEW COMPLETE, STORY DONE 2026-08-13. External Codex review (3-pass, high effort) found 1 High, 1 Medium and 5 Low; findings at specs/stories/code-review/oxp-1-codex-findings.md. The High was real and was independently reproduced before being trusted: the ISO-date pattern used bare \d{2} for month and day in BOTH office_seat.schema.js and seed-office-seats.mjs, so '2026-99-99' matched the shape and then parsed to Invalid Date - exactly the silent-NaN failure the pattern's own comment says it exists to prevent, on the very field (created_at) that feeds oxp.2's months-since-creation XP arithmetic. Fixed at both layers: the schema pattern now range-bounds month (0[1-9]|1[0-2]) and day (0[1-9]|[12]\d|3[01]), and the seed script adds a real calendar check. That check had to be stronger than Codex proposed: Date.parse alone is NOT enough, because V8 falls back to a lenient legacy parser and silently rolls '2026-02-30' forward to 2 March instead of returning NaN, so isRealCalendarDate does a UTC round-trip on the YYYY-MM-DD prefix AS WELL AS Date.parse on the whole string. The Medium was also real and was reproduced before patching: seedOfficeSeats was only sequentially idempotent (findOne then insertOne, with a gap), and four overlapping calls against an empty collection produced 13-19 documents where 7 were expected, reproducibly, with no unique index on the natural key to catch it. Replaced with a single atomic updateOne upsert on {office_category, holder_id} using $setOnInsert, which preserves the never-clobber-an-ST's-hand-edited-notes property (the match branch writes nothing) and the existing return shape. Two Low patched: main() now validates its arguments BEFORE calling connectDb(), so a zero-flag invocation fails without contacting live Atlas at all; and the N-seats tests now compile on genuinely separate Ajv instances (ajv.compile on the same instance and schema object returns the CACHED function, so the old 'second validator' comment was describing something that never happened). Two Low were record corrections: AC1's literal claim that OFFICE_DATA and character.schema.js share 'the same five values' was false (OFFICE_DATA has four and lacks Administrator), so AC1 now names character.schema.js as the authority and says why; and the Dev Agent Record's N-seat evidence claim was closed by STRENGTHENING the tests to match it rather than softening the prose (all three DB coexistence tests now re-validate every stored document, including the vacant seat, whose null holder must survive the JSON boundary as null and not as the string 'null'). The last Low was Codex correctly marking the limit of its own review and needed no action. Codex's unreproducible-gate finding was environmental (Mongo unreachable in its sandbox, EACCES, same pattern already recorded in otc-2/oaq-2/oxp-3/oxp-4): the exact six-file gate was re-run here with Mongo reachable and came back 121 passed / 121, 6 files, ZERO SKIPPED - which is 112 plus this round's 9 new tests, so the original 112/112 claim reproduces exactly rather than being dismissed. All five patches prove-discriminated by single-change revert, one at a time (the concurrency test was deliberately widened from two callers to four because at two it only caught the old shape on roughly one run in three; at four it caught it 5 times out of 5, while the upsert held at exactly 7 on 6 consecutive runs). STILL TRUE AND STILL IMPORTANT: the seed script has NEVER been run for real, by anyone, in either the dev session or the review round, and the live tm_suite database was never connected to or written to - every DB-touching test went through the vitest harness, whose tm_suite_test forcing was re-verified by reading the chain (vitest.config setupFiles -> setup-env.js -> assertTestDbSafety pre-connect -> setupDb post-connect recheck) rather than assumed. RENE'S DATE NOW CONFIRMED (2026-08-13): both live Primogen seats were created Game 1 (2026-02-21), same as Yusuf Kalusicj's - neither is "original" vs "later addition". Yusuf has only HELD his seat since Game 5 (a real prior handover, untracked by this holder-only collection, and irrelevant to the seat's own creation date). Only docs updated (seed script header, story seat table); RENE_PRIMOGEN_SEAT_CREATED_AT stays null in source, --rene-created-at=2026-02-21 is the confirmed value to pass at run time. Seed script still not run for real; that is Angelus's action to take, not an agent's.
978:  oxp-2-derived-office-xp-calculation: done                    # CODE REVIEW COMPLETE, STORY DONE 2026-08-13. External Codex review (3-pass, high effort, CLI-direct via codex exec): 0 High, 4 Medium, 6 Low (some describing the same underlying defect from two independent lenses). 4 patched - unanchored ISO-date regex letting malformed input like '2026-02-99' silently derive a plausible month figure (now anchored both ends, matching office_seat.schema.js's own isoDate pattern); officeSeatXp trusting allSeats to include the evaluated seat, so an incomplete array could make a real two-seat Primogen/Socialite category falsely report spendKnown:true (now fails safe to false, one-sided so well-formed calls are unaffected); the new route exposing notes (documented "ST caveats") to any player (Angelus's ruling, asked directly: redact notes for non-ST, keep everything else open, via the existing isStRole helper); AC7's route test only deep-checking two fields on one seat (now field-for-field on all seven). All four prove-discriminated by single-change revert. 3 Low deferred to deferred-work.md (no current consumer to misuse the accrual function's argument order; an O(n^2) recount nothing feels yet; a raw-document fallback gap confirmed unreachable via any real write path today). 6 new tests, 49 total (up from 43). Regression re-run with WORKING DB access this round (Codex's own sandbox hit the same transient Mongo EACCES already recorded for otc-2/oaq-2/oxp-1/oxp-3/oxp-4): 49/49 on oxp-2 itself, 111/112 on the four-file changed area, the 1 failure being the already-documented pre-existing oxp-4 source-slice issue. This also independently reproduced the dev-story agent's original mutation counts exactly (16 and 4 failures respectively), resolving Codex's own "unverifiable" flag on those numbers - its sandbox saw 14 and 3 because 9 DB-backed tests were skipped there, fully accounted for. Live tm_suite never touched. DEV COMPLETE NOTE follows, then ORIGINAL STORY-CREATION NOTE. Delivered: public/js/data/office-xp.js (officeMonthsAccrued / officeXpEarned / officeXpSpentForCategory / officeSpendKnownByCategory / officeSeatXp, plus the named OFFICE_XP_PER_MONTH rate), new read-only GET /api/office_seats (requireAuth + noCache, open read like both sibling office routes, no write verb at all), registered in server/index.js and in the supertest harness. New suite server/tests/oxp-2-derived-office-xp-calculation.test.js: 43 tests, 34 pure + 9 DB-backed against tm_suite_test, 43/43 passing with ZERO skipped (local mongod was reachable, so the DB block really ran). Office-domain regression 181/182 across 9 files; shared-helper regression 96/96 across 5 more. All four load-bearing gates prove-discriminated by single-change mutation, reverted one at a time: dropping the inclusive +1 failed 16, a 30-day-bucket day difference failed 16, defaulting spendKnown to true failed 4, an unconditional String() on holder_id failed 1 (the vacant seat). Angelus's multi-seat ruling implemented as scoped: earned XP is always exact per seat (the two Socialite seats correctly derive 7 vs 2), while spend/balance carry spendKnown:false for Primogen and Socialite - present as real numbers so nothing crashes on undefined, but documented in the code, the tests and the story as NOT attributable to an individual seat. TWO PRE-EXISTING FAILURES FOUND ON MAIN, neither caused by this story and neither fixed by it (both need an Angelus call): (1) oxp-1-office-seats.test.js does not load AT ALL - a bare SyntaxError with no location, isolated to the #!/usr/bin/env node shebang in server/scripts/seed-office-seats.mjs, which vitest's transform chokes on; all 41 oxp-1 tests are silently unrun, and the shebang has been there since the file's first commit, so oxp-1's own "121/121 zero skipped" record cannot be reproduced today (likely a vitest 4 upgrade since). This is why oxp-2's seven-seat fixture MIRRORS the OFFICE_SEATS table rather than importing buildSeatDocs. (2) oxp-4-merit-persistence-handover.test.js has 1 failure: its meritDotsBlock() source-slice now catches oxp.3's merged _adjustManoeuvreRank, whose comment contains the word "holder" - the guarantee is intact, the extraction window broke when oxp-3 and oxp-4 merged in sequence; proved pre-existing by stashing this story's tracked changes and re-running. Live tm_suite never connected to or written to. Story: oxp-2-derived-office-xp-calculation.md ORIGINAL STORY-CREATION NOTE: STORY CREATED 2026-08-13. Scoping investigation surfaced a real structural gap before ACs were written, ruled on directly by Angelus (same pattern as oxp-1's Finding 2): office_seats is seat-keyed (oxp-1) but office_merit_dots/office_manoeuvre_ranks are category-keyed (oxp-3/oxp-4) - so spend cannot currently be attributed per seat for Primogen/Socialite (2 live seats each). Ruling: earned XP always shown correctly per seat; spend/balance explicitly reported as undeterminable (spendKnown: false) for multi-seat categories rather than approximated or pooled. Confirmed live: office_merit_dots has 2 docs (Enforcer, Head of State - single-seat, unaffected), office_manoeuvre_ranks has 0 docs - the collision is real but not yet live-triggered. Accrual formula (calendar-month-inclusive, not day-based) verified against office-powers.md's own Feb-to-Aug-2026-equals-7 worked example. Scoped to: public/js/data/office-xp.js (pure calc functions), new GET /api/office_seats route, tests. Explicitly NOT migrating office_merit_dots/office_manoeuvre_ranks to seat-keying, NOT any UI consumption (oxp.6/oxp.7's job), NOT spend-approval routing (oxp.9), NOT handover/reset logic (oxp.5). Story: oxp-2-derived-office-xp-calculation.md
979:  oxp-3-manoeuvre-purchase-graduated-merit: done              # REVIEWED AND DONE 2026-08-13, merged to main via PR #1158. External Codex review (3-pass isolated, high effort) against the review state: 0 High, 5 Medium, 4 Low, every finding independently reproduced before patching. 3 Medium patched - (1) fail-open manoeuvre list on a rejected rank fetch (the synchronous render deliberately mutes nothing while the rank is unknown, so a silent failure left the holder looking at five active manoeuvres; the list is now replaced with a Could-not-load-purchase-state message, matching _wireMeritDots's own catch idiom in the sibling section); (2) stale-category repaint - an adjustment resolving after a category switch re-queried the tab root and painted the OLD office's rank, controls and rows into the NEW one's markup; closed with a render-generation counter on el itself (el._officeManoeuvreGen, bumped in renderOfficeTab, captured before the first await, checked before every DOM write and before the post-write re-render), adapting office-approvals.js's module-scoped _fetchGen precedent to an element that is re-rendered repeatedly; (3) lost-update race on concurrent stepper clicks - _adjustManoeuvreRank did a client-side read-compute-write, so two overlapping adjustments both read the same rank and both wrote the same next value; fixed SERVER-side with a new atomic PUT /:category/step (requireRole('st'), body {delta}) doing increment + [0, manoeuvres.length] clamp in one aggregation-pipeline findOneAndUpdate with upsert, and the client now sends only the signed delta. The absolute-set PUT /:category is kept deliberately (set-to-exactly-N has correct last-writer-wins semantics; only the stepper needed the relative form). 1 Medium ruled NOT a bug by Angelus - an ST/dev seeing the rank readout while browsing a reference office is intended, matching the Merit Suite's already-shipped isOwnOffice-independent stepper and the set-up-before-handover case AC6 was written for; AC2 clarified to state it governs the MANOEUVRE LIST (unchanged, and structurally clean: the muted class is never emitted in reference view at all, so a browsing player's DOM carries no purchase state), the readout being AC6's widget with a deliberate ST/dev exemption. 1 Medium (all-8-ACs claim overstated without AC7's concurrency outcome) closed by patch 3; AC7 rewritten off the re-fetch-before-write pattern it originally mandated, since that pattern was itself the defect. 2 Low patched (clamp inside the exported manoeuvreRankHtml, which threw RangeError on a negative rank; five-existing-tests miscount corrected to four), 1 Low superseded by 3 new fake-DOM tests driving the real async wiring end to end (no jsdom in this project - same hand-rolled technique as otc.3's fake <select>, with localStorage and fetch stubbed the way this file's header already stubs location), 1 Low dismissed as environmental: Codex's 9-files/107-passed/49-skipped gate result came from a wrong filename in the reviewer-supplied command (tests/oaq-2-pending-status-actions-accept-decline.test.js, real file tests/oaq-2-pending-status-actions.test.js) plus Mongo EACCES from an orphaned concurrent codex exec process; after killing it and correcting the filename the same gate re-ran clean at 171/171 across 10/10 files, matching the dev round - same transient-reachability pattern already recorded in otc-2/oaq-2, both readings accurate for their own moment. All 3 fixes prove-discriminated by single-change revert (2 / 1 / 3 expected failures respectively, nothing else moving; fix 3 verified over three consecutive runs, its concurrency test firing FOUR simultaneous steps rather than two because two racers did not reliably interleave). Final regression 186/186 across 10 files, zero skipped, up from 171. No unresolved High/Medium. Story: oxp-3-manoeuvre-purchase-graduated-merit.md
980:  oxp-4-merit-purchase-persists-handover: done                # REVIEWED AND DONE 2026-08-13, merged to main via PR #1159. External Codex review (3-pass single-session, high effort) against the review state: 0 High, 1 Medium, 2 Low. The Medium was environmental only - Codex's own sandbox hit EACCES connecting to Mongo, skipping all DB-backed tests and leaving its "38/38" claim unverifiable from inside its run; dismissed by an independent fresh re-run on this machine immediately after, 38/38 with 0 skipped, matching the dev round exactly (same transient-reachability pattern already recorded for otc-2/oaq-2/oxp-3). 1 Low accepted as a standing limitation (the client-wiring "never references a character" test is name-pattern-based, not semantic - this project has no DOM/browser harness, so every client-wiring test here is a static source contract by construction; the real backstop is AC1/AC2's server-side proof). 1 Low patched: FIXTURE_PREFIX was interpolated into a Mongo $regex unescaped in the new test file's cleanup hooks - harmless today, latent if the prefix constant ever gained a regex metacharacter; added a FIXTURE_PREFIX_RE escaped once at module scope, both deleteMany calls switched to it, re-verified 38/38. Proof-and-document story delivered as scoped: new DB-backed test file (10 tests, 5 server + 5 client source-contract), plus two explanatory comments on office-merit-dots.js's _id: category keying. No new mechanism, no reset logic, no touch to the deferred lost-update race. Task 2's UI-clarity verdict was an honest "no copy change needed" (and one now would be wrong until oxp.5 makes merits and manoeuvres actually differ) - reasoning in the story's Dev Agent Record and Senior Developer Review. Final regression 38/38, 0 skipped, no unresolved High/Medium. Story: oxp-4-merit-purchase-persists-handover.md ORIGINAL STORY-CREATION NOTE: STORY CREATED 2026-08-13. Investigation done during story-writing: office_merit_dots (PR #1147) is keyed purely by office category (_id: category), no character/holder reference anywhere in read or write path; court_category itself has no history/handover-detection mechanism anywhere in this codebase. Conclusion: merit persistence-across-handover is ALREADY TRUE by construction, not something to build - this story is a proof-and-document story (new DB-backed test simulating a handover, a client-wiring test pinning that no holder id ever reaches the merit-dots API, an explanatory code comment on the _id: category keying), not a new-mechanism story. Deliberately does NOT touch the pre-existing office-merit-dots lost-update race (separate concern, already logged in deferred-work.md with oxp-3's fix shape to mirror) or oxp.5's manoeuvre-reset logic. Story: oxp-4-merit-purchase-persists-handover.md
981:  oxp-5-handover-logic: done                                  # CODE REVIEW COMPLETE, STORY DONE 2026-08-14 (external Codex review via the codex-review skill; full outcome at the end of this entry and in oxp-5-handover-logic.md's Senior Developer Review section). STILL NOT COMMITTED, NOT pushed, NOT merged - Angelus's call. DEV COMPLETE 2026-08-13, awaiting code review, NOT committed at that point. Delivered: new transactional PUT /api/office_seats/:seatId/holder on the already-mounted office-seats router (no server/index.js or test-app.js change), requireRole('st') on the write while GET / stays open - the same split office-merit-dots.js uses. Transaction scaffolding copied from office-actions.js's PUT /:id/accept: getClient() + startSession() + withTransaction, a locally-defined RouteResponse extends Error so a business rejection aborts cleanly without the driver's transient-error retry, status/body captured outside the callback, response sent after commit, endSession in a finally. Write order inside the callback is read seat, read target, AC2 conflict check, CAS-claim the seat FIRST, clear the departing holder (matchedCount 0 is benign and reported, never an abort), set the incoming holder, reset the manoeuvre rank LAST. holder_id is stored as a real ObjectId, pinned by test. ONE DESIGN DEVIATION FROM THE STORY TEXT, made deliberately and worth a reviewer's attention: AC3 puts the seat read inside the transaction, and the compare-and-swap baseline is instead captured by an ADDITIONAL read taken BEFORE the transaction opens (the seat is still re-read inside it, for the 404-on-delete and the authoritative category). Reason, found empirically rather than reasoned about in the abstract: session.withTransaction RE-RUNS its callback on any error MongoDB labels transient, and a genuine concurrent write to the same document is exactly that (WriteConflict). With the CAS filtering on a holder read INSIDE the callback, the retry re-reads, sees the winner's freshly committed holder, filters on THAT, and succeeds - so two simultaneous handovers would both return 200 and the ladder would be reset twice. The outer read freezes the baseline across retries so the loser's filter names the pre-race holder, matches nothing, and 409s. This mirrors office-actions.js's own _findPending, which likewise reads its record outside the transaction and uses that outer value in the CAS inside it. A SECOND, SMALLER DEVIATION: AC6 specifies updateOne; the implementation uses findOneAndUpdate with returnDocument 'before'. It is the identical single atomic aggregation-pipeline update (the same idiom office-manoeuvre-rank.js's step route uses) and additionally returns the pre-image, which is the only way to satisfy AC1's response requirement (rank before the reset, new cumulative destroyed total) without adding a second read. TESTING NOTE THE STORY GOT WRONG, corrected by measurement: AC10's mandated "two simultaneous handovers produce exactly one 200 and one 409" is NOT a reliable assertion, because two requests fired through Promise.all do not reliably interleave - measured at roughly 4 times in 5 on this machine, and in the fifth the second request's pre-read lands after the first has committed, making it two SEQUENTIAL handovers for which two 200s is the correct answer, not a bug. Asserting a bare "exactly one 200" would have been asserting the scheduler, i.e. a flaky test. Replaced with a 10-iteration loop (issue-1143's own convention, and the same reason oxp-3's review had to widen its race from two callers to four) asserting the invariants that hold either way: every response is a 200 or a clean 409 and never a crash; two winners are only ever legitimate sequential handovers, provable because they report DIFFERENT previous_holder_id values (two winners reporting the SAME prior holder is the real bug and never occurs); the sitting holder's 3-XP ladder is destroyed EXACTLY once, counter landing on 3 and never 6, which is "the loser destroyed no XP" in its non-flaky form; exactly one rival ends up holding the seat with a matching court_category and the other holds nothing; and at least one iteration must actually produce a 409, so the guard is proved to fire rather than never being reached. CLIENT: admin/city-views.js's court panel is now seat-backed - seats fetched once in initCityView beside terrDocs (renderCity/renderCourt stay synchronous), one row per real seat carrying data-seat-id, ordered ascending created_at then _id to match office-tab.js's _fallbackSeat exactly, seat_label or a short id shown when a category has more than one seat, an explicit no-seat line for a category with none, and saveCourt making ONE handover call per changed row with NO /api/characters/ call anywhere in that path. The Add-slot and remove-slot buttons and their handlers are removed entirely (see the story: they created a holder with no seat behind them). apiPut was confirmed to surface the server's own message body, so a 409 arrives naming the conflicting seat; two supporting changes were needed to make it legible - the panel's open/closed state is now remembered across a re-render (court-save-status lives INSIDE the panel, which renderCourt otherwise re-renders collapsed, so the message would have been written into a hidden element) and the save now re-renders BEFORE writing the status text, #634's ordering, which also fixes a pre-existing bug where the old "Saved" message was wiped in the same tick. TESTS: new server/tests/oxp-5-handover-logic.test.js, 46 tests (seat fixture decade 51-56, escaped fixture-prefix regex at module scope), 46/46. FULL TARGETED GATE, all seven files in one invocation: 204/204, 0 failed, run THREE consecutive times identically, with a verbose pass counting 204 individually-ticked tests so nothing was skipped rather than passing. TWO BUGS FOUND IN VERIFICATION AFTER THE FIRST DEV PASS REPORTED GREEN, both written up in full in the story's Dev Agent Record. Bug 1, real and fixed: this suite's beforeEach/afterAll ran UNFILTERED deleteMany({}) on office_merit_dots and office_manoeuvre_ranks. The story text spells the hazard out for office_seats and that was followed, but the identical reasoning applies to the two purchase collections (same seat keys, same five sharing suites) and was missed; all four deletes are now one removeFixtures() helper scoped to this suite's own seat ids, and the one no-document-minted assertion that counted with a bare {} filter moved to the same scope. Bug 2, NOT a bug in this story: verification saw 24 failed/180 passed across 4 of 7 files, non-deterministic failure counts (12/8/10) across three supposedly-isolated runs, an E11000 duplicate-key on seat 51 thrown from this suite's own beforeEach insert, and intermittent failures in PURE STATIC source-contract tests that touch no database. That last symptom is the thread: beforeEach is registered at FILE level, so when it throws vitest fails every test in the file including the static ones, meaning there was one fault and not many. Root cause, PROVED not assumed: two vitest processes were running against the same tm_suite_test database at once (the dev run and the verification run). setup-env.js redirects only the database NAME, tm_suite_test is a single shared database on the same Atlas replica set as production with no per-run namespacing, and nothing detects a second concurrent run. Proof in three steps - beforeEach temporarily instrumented to report seat count before cleanup, deletedCount, and count immediately after; run alone three consecutive times 46/46 with the diagnostic never firing, and the seven-file gate alone 204/204 likewise; then two full gates deliberately launched concurrently, reproducing the original signature immediately (68 failed/136 passed in one process, 67/137 in the other) with diagnostics impossible from a single process - DIAG pre=6 deleted=0 mid=6 (this run's deleteMany removed nothing because the other run had already deleted the seats, yet a count taken immediately after found all six back) and DIAG INSERT FAIL pre=6 deleted=6 mid=0 present=[all six] (all six deleted, verified absent, all six present again microseconds later at the insert). So: no async leak, no unjoined Promise.all, no transaction outliving its response, and nothing wrong with the route - endSession and the commit are genuinely settled before each HTTP response resolves, and the AC3 concurrency test was not the culprit and needed no change. The harness limitation is real, pre-existing and unguarded, sits alongside the isDbAvailable connectivity-not-transaction-support caveat, and matters in this workspace specifically because it is shared between concurrent sessions: only ONE vitest process may run against tm_suite_test at a time, and what a second one produces looks exactly like a subtle application bug. Fixing the harness (per-run database namespacing, or a lock) was deliberately not attempted here. oxp-2's "exposes no write verb" test restated and STRENGTHENED per AC10 (collection root still refuses all four verbs; a bare seat id is not addressable either; the /holder sub-route answers to PUT alone) rather than left to rot, and oxp-4's repointSeat comment corrected while the helper deliberately keeps writing the seat directly so its merits-survive proof stays independent of this route rather than circular. All three mandated prove-discrimination checks run as single changes, one at a time, each reverted and re-confirmed green: inverting AC6's two pipeline stages failed exactly the 5 destroyed-counter tests; removing AC2's conflict check failed exactly the 3 refusal tests; disabling the AC4 same-holder branch failed exactly the 5 no-op tests. Nothing unrelated moved in any of the three. FLAGGED FOR ANGELUS, a decision he may want to turn into a story: removing the Add-slot control leaves IN-APP SEAT CREATION AND DELETION as a real gap with no story home - seats are still minted only by oxp-1's manual seed script, which has still never been run for real. Live tm_suite was never connected to or written to. Pre-existing and untouched: oxp-1-office-seats.test.js still does not load at all under vitest (the shebang in seed-office-seats.mjs), so its 41 tests remain silently unrun. ORIGINAL STORY-CREATION NOTE FOLLOWS. STORY CREATED 2026-08-13. Implements office-powers.md's handover ruling: on a change of officeholder, permanent merits stay with the office and manoeuvres reset to zero with their spent XP destroyed (not refunded, not banked). Nothing in this codebase implements the reset, and nothing records a handover at all - characters.court_category (which actually gates behaviour: office-actions.js's auth check, office-tab.js's isOwnOffice, city-tab.js, admin/city-views.js) and office_seats.holder_id (which oxp-2 and oxp-11 depend on to resolve WHICH seat's purchase state to show) are two independent facts that agree today purely by accident. HARD REQUIREMENT INHERITED FROM oxp-11, now satisfied by AC3: oxp-5 MUST write office_seats.holder_id when a seat changes hands, or the new holder stops resolving to their own seat by holder match and a multi-seat office (Primogen, Socialite) can resolve to the WRONG seat. Also UNBLOCKED by oxp-11: both purchase collections are seat-keyed, so resetting one seat's manoeuvre rank no longer destroys the other seat's unrelated progress in the same office. ANGELUS'S TWO SCOPING RULINGS, asked directly before ACs were written (2026-08-13): (1) do NOT derive court_category from holder_id - that is a large, risky refactor touching every read site; instead build ONE transactional route that keeps the two in sync atomically and leaves every existing read site untouched, which is the smaller option and the reason this is one story; (2) DO rewire admin/city-views.js's existing court-slots panel to call it, since leaving that panel as a raw-edit path would let an ST bypass the entire story through the familiar control. THREE FINDINGS FROM THIS STORY'S OWN INVESTIGATION, none of them in the original scoping, each confirmed against the real code. FINDING 1, the most important: the reset implemented the obvious way would REFUND the destroyed XP rather than destroying it. Office spend is DERIVED, never stored - oxp-2's officeXpSpentForCategory sums current merit dots plus the current manoeuvre rank - so zeroing rank makes derived spend DROP and the balance RISE by exactly the amount the ruling says must be gone. office-powers.md anticipated this ("the running balance is total accrued since creation, minus everything ever spent, INCLUDING THE SPEND THAT HAS SINCE BEEN LOST") and it cannot be recomputed after the fact by any means, so AC6 captures a cumulative manoeuvre_xp_destroyed counter on the seat's office_manoeuvre_ranks document in the same atomic aggregation-pipeline update that zeroes the rank (stage order is load-bearing: stage 1 must read the original $rank before stage 2 sets it to 0). The ARITHMETIC is deliberately handed forward rather than wired here, exactly as oxp-11 handed holder_id to this story: office-xp.js has no consumer yet so nothing is wrong on screen today, and changing its functions would rewrite oxp-2's AC8 from inside this story - but whoever builds oxp-6/oxp-7 MUST add manoeuvre_xp_destroyed to officeXpSpentForCategory before rendering any balance, or it over-reports by the destroyed amount. FINDING 2: resolveOfficeSeat() in server/lib/office-seat-resolve.js CANNOT be reused, for two independent reasons - it 400s any seat whose office has no OFFICE_DATA entry, which is Administrator until oxp-8, and the Administrator seat is real, filled (Ivana Horvat, since Game 5) and must be handoverable; and it takes no session parameter so it would read outside the transaction. Only its exported SEAT_ID_PATTERN is reused, and a test pins that an Administrator handover succeeds so the refusal cannot creep back in via a later tidy-up. FINDING 3: court_title lives on the CHARACTER and seat_label on the SEAT, and they are not equivalents - seat_label is the seat's permanent distinguisher ("Harpy" appointed vs "People's Harpy" popular) and is the only thing telling Socialite's two seats apart, so the route writes court_title and must NEVER write seat_label (AC8 pins it). SCOPE: new PUT /api/office_seats/:seatId/holder on the existing (already-mounted, so no index.js or test-app.js change) office-seats router, requireRole('st'), body {holder_id: 24-hex or null, court_title optional}, wrapped in getClient()+session.withTransaction() copying office-actions.js's PUT /:id/accept pattern exactly - RouteResponse extends Error for clean business rejections that do not trigger the driver's transient-error retry, claim-the-seat-FIRST with a compare-and-swap on its current holder_id (matchedCount 0 gives 409), status/body captured outside the callback and sent after commit. Write sequence: read seat, read target, refuse-on-conflicting-seat, CAS-claim the seat, clear the departing holder (CAS-filtered on this office's category; matchedCount 0 is BENIGN, meaning they had already moved on, not an error), set the incoming holder, then reset the manoeuvre rank last. holder_id is stored as a real ObjectId, never a string (live storage is BSON ObjectIds; a string would create data-map.md Known Drift Pattern #2). AC2 REFUSE-ON-CONFLICT: a target already holding a DIFFERENT seat is a 409 naming the conflicting seat and requiring an explicit vacate first, never a silent cascade - court_category is a single field, so assigning someone into a second seat would either leave the first seat's holder_id stale (oxp-11's bug relocated, not solved) or make this route silently modify a third document the caller never named; same refuse-rather-than-guess posture as oxp-1's seed script and oxp-11's migration. A character with a court_category but NO seat behind them is explicitly NOT a conflict - the seat is authoritative and the stale value is overwritten. AC4: same-holder (including null-to-null re-vacate) is a safe idempotent 200 no-op with handover false, no reset and no XP destroyed, because the court panel saves every slot and re-saving must never be able to wipe a ladder; a changed court_title is still applied. AC5: vacating (holder_id null) IS a handover and destroys the departing holder's manoeuvre investment exactly as a replacement would - the ruling's own consequence 2 (a holder near the end of their tenure has no reason to buy manoeuvres) depends on it. AC6 reset uses upsert false deliberately, so a seat that never purchased a rank is a correct silent no-op rather than minting a meaningless rank-0 document, and sets rank to 0 rather than deleting the document so updated_at and the destroyed counter survive as evidence a reset happened. AC7: office_merit_dots is untouched and PROVED so (byte-identical including updated_at, in the same test that proves the rank DID reset) - this matters more than it did for oxp-4, whose guarantee was safe only because nothing COULD reset anything; this story builds the thing that can. CLIENT (AC9): admin/city-views.js's court panel becomes SEAT-BACKED. Investigation found its slot rows have NO seat identity at all - a row is keyed only by category and DOM order, and saveCourt does a clear-pass then an assign-pass of independent PUT /api/characters/:id calls - so this is a rework, not a redirect of two URLs. Seats are fetched once in initCityView beside terrDocs (renderCity/renderCourt stay synchronous), one row per seat carrying data-seat-id, ordered ascending created_at then _id to MATCH oxp-11's _fallbackSeat so the admin panel and the office tab never disagree about which seat is first, with seat_label or a short id shown so Primogen's two identically-titled seats are distinguishable, and an explicit no-seat line for a category with no seat. saveCourt then makes ONE handover call per changed row and NO /api/characters/ call at all - the new route's single call replaces both halves of a reassignment. The Add-slot and remove-slot buttons are REMOVED rather than rewired: they currently create a holding by writing court_category to an extra character, producing a holder with no seat behind them, invisible to oxp-2's XP derivation and oxp-11's purchase resolution - i.e. exactly the inconsistent data this story exists to stop. THAT LEAVES IN-APP SEAT CREATION/DELETION AS A REAL GAP WITH NO STORY HOME - seats are still created only by oxp-1's manual seed script. Adding a story for it was not in this story's brief; it is Angelus's call. AC10: oxp-2-derived-office-xp-calculation.test.js's "exposes no write verb - this story adds a GET and nothing else" test will still PASS mechanically (the new route is at /:seatId/holder, so a bare PUT /api/office_seats still 404s) while its title and comment become false, so it is restated and STRENGTHENED (collection root has no write verb; the seat-scoped route accepts only PUT) rather than left to rot; oxp-4's repointSeat helper comment is corrected too, but the helper deliberately keeps writing the seat directly so its merits-survive proof stays independent of this story's route rather than circular. TESTING: transactions genuinely work here - this repo never runs against a standalone mongod (db.js hard-codes tls true against an Atlas mongodb+srv cluster and setup-env.js redirects only the DATABASE NAME to tm_suite_test, so it is the same replica set as production), there is no memory-server and no replica-set detection anywhere, and the existing transactional-route suites (oaq-2, otc-2) do not gate on it at all. isDbAvailable() is a CONNECTIVITY skip only. Concurrency proved by the established Promise.all race convention (oaq-2 AC8, issue-1143): two simultaneous handovers on one seat, exactly one 200 and one 409, loser destroys no XP. Seat fixtures use the namespaced deterministic-ObjectId decade convention (oxp-3 took 31-34, oxp-4 took 41-43; this suite claims 51+) and must NEVER deleteMany({}) office_seats, which three other suites share. NOT IN SCOPE: no backfill of the 7 live seats (already fully consistent per oxp-11's read-only check), no change to any court_category read site, no office_merit_dots change, no office-tab.js change (oxp-11's _wirePurchaseState improves for free once holder_id is maintained: its confirmed flag becomes true for real holders and its could-not-confirm-which-seat-is-yours disclosure stops firing for them), no office-xp.js change, no seat CRUD, no richer handover UX (oxp-6 owns the seat picker), no oxp-9 spend-approval routing, no oxp-10 OFFICE_DATA migration. Story: oxp-5-handover-logic.md CODE REVIEW OUTCOME, 2026-08-14 (this session picked up a prior session's mid-flight review return - findings had already landed and city-views.js/office-seats.js were already partly patched, but bookkeeping was not closed out, so every finding was independently re-verified against the running code first): 1 High + 6 Medium PATCHED, 3 Medium DISMISSED with evidence (already correctly designed and already justified in this story's own pre-review Dev Notes, which the review's frozen-pass methodology could not revise against), 1 Medium INFORMATIONAL (reviewer's sandbox denied Atlas), 1 Medium DEFERRED to deferred-work.md (transaction-rollback fault-injection - a real gap, but with no precedent anywhere in this codebase including the office-actions.js route this one copied), 2 Low PATCHED, 1 Low DEFERRED. The High: courtSlotOptions built its holder <select> from ACTIVE characters only, so a seat held by a RETIRED character had no matching option, silently defaulted to Vacant, and an UNRELATED save on that row fired a real handover - destroying the retired holder's manoeuvre XP permanently. Fixed, with new direct unit suite server/tests/oxp-5-city-views-seat-holder.test.js (13 tests, prove-discriminated by single-change revert) covering seatHolder/courtSlotOptions/computeCourtChanges directly - the first tests in this codebase to import and call city-views.js functions rather than only pinning its source by regex. Re-verified targeted gate (now eight files): 217/217, 0 failed, 0 skipped. Full finding-by-finding triage at specs/stories/code-review/oxp-5-codex-findings.md.
982:  oxp-6-office-tab-purchase-markers: backlog                  # HARD REQUIREMENT HANDED FORWARD BY oxp-5 (2026-08-13), do not rediscover this the hard way: officeXpSpentForCategory in public/js/data/office-xp.js MUST add office_manoeuvre_ranks.manoeuvre_xp_destroyed to its spend total BEFORE any balance is rendered anywhere. Office spend is DERIVED from current state (current merit dots + current manoeuvre rank), so oxp-5's handover reset drops the derived spend by the destroyed rank and RAISES the balance by exactly the amount content/rules/office-powers.md says is gone - a refund, which the ruling forbids explicitly ("the running balance is total accrued since creation, minus everything ever spent, INCLUDING THE SPEND THAT HAS SINCE BEEN LOST"). oxp-5 stores the cumulative counter and deliberately does not consume it, because office-xp.js has no consumer yet so nothing is wrong on screen today, and wiring it there would have rewritten oxp-2's AC8 from inside oxp-5. The counter cannot be recomputed from anything at any later time by any means - it is destroyed by definition and exists nowhere else - so if a balance ships without it, the number is simply wrong and there is no way to detect it after the fact. LIMITATION INHERITED FROM oxp-11 (2026-08-13): an ST or dev browsing a MULTI-SEAT office they do not hold (Primogen, Socialite) sees ONE of its seats' purchase state, chosen by a deterministic fallback (ascending created_at then _id), and can edit it with the stepper. oxp-11 ships an on-screen note naming which seat is on screen, which makes that survivable rather than dangerous, but it is a label and not a picker - Angelus scoped oxp-11 as "backend plus minimal client wiring only". A real seat picker belongs HERE, and oxp-6 will need one for its own reasons anyway. Full reference list, unpurchased greyed/muted (reuse .pointed.hollow). Distinguish "no pool XP" from "rank not reached" via reason on tap/hover, not one visual state. SCOPING SESSION 2026-08-13 (while oxp-5's dev-story ran, ahead of ACs): investigated office-tab.js's real current state - _wireMeritDots/manoeuvreRankHtml still hand-build raw Unicode dots, not the codebase's standard .pointed/.pointed.hollow CSS dots (components.css:55-56) that Merit Suite and attribute/skill rows already use; NOTHING in office-tab.js computes affordability today, it is a pure ST admin stepper wholly disconnected from office-xp.js's balance functions. ANGELUS'S RULING, asked directly: the seat picker and the new purchase/affordability markers stay HOLDER-OR-ST-ONLY. otc.3's explicit privacy design ("a reference viewer's DOM carries no purchase state at all") is NOT widened by this story - a player browsing another office as reference keeps seeing the existing plain, unmuted, purchase-state-free summary; nothing new here is visible to them. The seat picker itself is therefore an ST/dev tool for choosing which of a multi-seat office's seats to view/edit as reference, not a self-identification aid for an uncertain holder - and note that need shrinks going forward once oxp-5 ships, since every future handover then keeps holder_id current and the deterministic-fallback ambiguity oxp-11 flagged mostly stops recurring for NEW handovers (it can still bite on any seat whose holder_id predates oxp-5, which is none of the 7 live seats per oxp-11's own read-only check). HARD DEPENDENCY ON oxp-5's Finding 1: distinguishing "no pool XP" from "rank not reached" requires a real balance, and oxp-5's manoeuvre_xp_destroyed counter has to be added into office-xp.js's officeXpSpentForCategory before that balance is honest - oxp-5's own Dev Notes name oxp-6 (or oxp-7) as the first real consumer required to do this wiring, so it is IN this story's scope, not assumed pre-done. Dev-story should not start until oxp-5 is merged to main.
983:  oxp-7-sheet-office-merits-section: backlog                  # New sheet section parallel to existing Domain Merits pattern (sheet.js:1122); holder-only. SCOPING SESSION 2026-08-13 (while oxp-5's dev-story ran, ahead of ACs): "parallel to Domain Merits" can only mean visually/structurally similar, not the same data path - Domain Merits reads merits the character owns directly off c.merits, while office merit dots live in office_merit_dots keyed by SEAT and are fetched over the API (office-tab.js's existing _wireMeritDots pattern), so this section has no local character-document source to read synchronously the way shRenderDomainMerits does. ANGELUS'S RULING, asked directly: READ-ONLY. office-tab.js stays the one editable surface for office_merit_dots; this section does not carry the +/- ST steppers office-tab.js already has, so office_merit_dots keeps exactly one write path rather than gaining a second that could race or drift against it. "Holder-only" (not yet asked in detail) most naturally parallels shRenderDomainMerits's own gating (renders only when the viewed character currently holds a seat, i.e. char.court_category is set) and the story's own name scopes it to MERITS only, not manoeuvres - confirm both when ACs are written, not assumed beyond what is recorded here.
